\title{Codiscrete Bicategories of Unital Magmas\\ and the Combinatorics of the Associativity Defect}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We attach to every pointed magma $M$ --- a set with an entirely arbitrary binary operation $\ast$ and an arbitrary distinguished element $1$, subject to no axioms --- a one-object bicategory $\Bic(M)$ whose hom-category is the \emph{codiscrete} category on $M$, with horizontal composition given by $\ast$ and identity $1$-cell given by $1$. Because the hom-category is thin and total, the associator $(a\ast b)\ast c \cong a\ast (b\ast c)$ and the unitors $1\ast a \cong a$, $a \ast 1 \cong a$ exist and are invertible unconditionally, and Mac Lane's pentagon and triangle identities hold automatically; thus arbitrary unit and associativity defects are converted into coherent invertible $2$-cells with no hypotheses whatsoever. We then delimit exactly what this construction preserves and what it forgets: $\Bic(M)$ is strict if and only if $M$ is a monoid; every ordered pair of $1$-cells forms an adjoint equivalence; but a $1$-cell is \emph{strictly} invertible if and only if the corresponding element has a two-sided inverse; and every set map $M\to N$, with no compatibility hypothesis, induces a pseudofunctor, functorially, which is strictly multiplicative precisely for magma homomorphisms. The second half quantifies the weakness. For a finite magma we define the associativity defect $D(M)=\#\{(a,b,c):(a\ast b)\ast c\neq a\ast (b\ast c)\}$ and prove: $D(M)$ equals the number of non-identity associator instances of $\Bic(M)$, so that for unital $M$ the bicategory is a $2$-category iff $D(M)=0$; the associative-triple count is multiplicative under products; $D$ is invariant under isomorphism, reversal and free unitalisation; $D$ is even for finite commutative magmas; and the sharp bounds $D\le (n-1)^3$ for unital magmas of order $n$ and $D\le (n-1)^3-(n-1)^2$ in the commutative unital case, both attained --- by the shift magma of a fixed-point-free self-map and by the negation magma of a $2$-torsion-free abelian group.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} magma, bicategory, codiscrete category, coherence, associator, associativity defect, extremal combinatorics, parity involution.

\section{Introduction}

Associativity is an axiom, not a fact. It is imposed on semigroups, monoids and groups because it makes the theory work, but many natural binary operations violate it: subtraction, exponentiation, the vector cross product, the arithmetic mean, the octonion product, floating-point addition, and --- most relevant here --- an arbitrary finite multiplication table produced by search or by a physical process.

Two complementary responses are available. The \emph{categorical response} weakens equality: instead of demanding $(a\ast b)\ast c=a\ast(b\ast c)$, one supplies a specified isomorphism $\alpha_{a,b,c}$ and asks it to be coherent, meaning that all diagrams built from these isomorphisms commute. The archetype is the theory of monoidal categories and bicategories, where Mac Lane's coherence theorem reduces the infinitely many required commutativities to the pentagon and triangle identities. The \emph{combinatorial response} measures: count the triples at which the law fails, and study that number as a statistic of the table.

This paper carries out both and connects them by an exact identity. Section~\ref{sec:prelim} fixes notation; Section~\ref{sec:constr} constructs the codiscrete bicategory of an arbitrary pointed magma and establishes automatic coherence; Section~\ref{sec:forget} determines what is preserved and what is forgotten; Section~\ref{sec:defect} develops the combinatorics of the defect with sharp bounds and extremal constructions; Section~\ref{sec:bridge} exhibits the bridge; Section~\ref{sec:enum} records exhaustive enumerations for small orders, including a refuted conjecture; Sections~\ref{sec:algo}--\ref{sec:future} discuss algorithms, applications and open problems.

The guiding slogan, made precise from opposite directions by the two halves, is: \emph{coherence is free; information is not.}

\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:prelim}

\begin{definition}[Magma, pointed magma]
A \emph{magma} is a set $M$ with a binary operation $\ast:M\times M\to M$; no axioms are imposed. A \emph{pointed magma} is a magma with a distinguished element $1\in M$, again with no axioms. It is \emph{unital} if $1\ast a=a=a\ast 1$ for all $a$, and a \emph{monoid} if in addition $\ast$ is associative.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Codiscrete category]
For a set $A$, the \emph{codiscrete} category $\Cod(A)$ has object set $A$ and exactly one morphism $x\to y$ for every ordered pair $(x,y)$; composition and identities are forced. $\Cod(A)$ is a \emph{thin groupoid}: every morphism is invertible and any two parallel morphisms are equal.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bicategory]
A bicategory consists of objects; for each ordered pair of objects a category of $1$-cells and $2$-cells; horizontal composition functors; identity $1$-cells; and invertible natural transformations $\alpha_{f,g,h}:(f\circ g)\circ h\Rightarrow f\circ(g\circ h)$, $\lambda_f:\id\circ f\Rightarrow f$, $\rho_f:f\circ\id\Rightarrow f$, subject to the pentagon identity for four composable $1$-cells and the triangle identity relating $\alpha$ to $\lambda,\rho$. It is \emph{strict} (a $2$-category) when $\alpha,\lambda,\rho$ are identities, equivalently when composition of $1$-cells is associative and unital on the nose.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Adjoint equivalence, pseudofunctor]
A $1$-cell $f:X\to Y$ is part of an \emph{adjoint equivalence} if there are $g:Y\to X$ and invertible $2$-cells $\eta:\id_X\Rightarrow g\circ f$, $\varepsilon:f\circ g\Rightarrow \id_Y$ satisfying the two triangle identities. A \emph{pseudofunctor} assigns objects, $1$-cells and $2$-cells functorially in each hom-category, together with invertible comparison $2$-cells $F(\id_X)\cong\id_{FX}$ and $F(f)\circ F(g)\cong F(f\circ g)$ compatible with associators and unitors.
\end{definition}

\section{The codiscrete bicategory of a pointed magma}\label{sec:constr}

Throughout, $(M,\ast,1)$ is an arbitrary pointed magma.

\begin{definition}[Codiscrete magma bicategory]\label{def:bic}
Let $\Bic(M)$ consist of: a single object $\star$; hom-category $\Bic(M)(\star,\star)=\Cod(M)$, whose objects (the $1$-cells) are the elements of $M$ --- written $\cell{a}$ for $a\in M$ --- with exactly one $2$-cell $\cell{a}\Rightarrow\cell{b}$ for every ordered pair; horizontal composition $\cell{a}\circ\cell{b}=\cell{a\ast b}$; identity $1$-cell $\id_\star=\cell{1}$; and associator and unitors the unique $2$-cells
\[
\cell{(a\ast b)\ast c}\Rightarrow\cell{a\ast(b\ast c)},\qquad
\cell{1\ast a}\Rightarrow\cell{a},\qquad
\cell{a\ast 1}\Rightarrow\cell{a}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Thinness]\label{lem:thin}
Any two parallel $2$-cells of $\Bic(M)$ are equal, and every $2$-cell is invertible; hence $\Bic(M)(\star,\star)$ is a groupoid.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both statements follow from codiscreteness. The hom-set between any two $1$-cells is a singleton, so parallel $2$-cells coincide; the unique $2$-cell in the reverse direction is a two-sided inverse, since both composites are parallel to the relevant identity and therefore equal to it.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Automatic coherence]\label{thm:coh}
For every pointed magma $M$ the data of Definition~\ref{def:bic} form a bicategory. The associator and unitors are natural and invertible, and the pentagon and triangle identities hold. Every diagram of $2$-cells in $\Bic(M)$ commutes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each bicategory axiom --- functoriality of whiskering, interchange, naturality of $\alpha,\lambda,\rho$, pentagon, triangle --- is an equation between two $2$-cells with the same source and target $1$-cell, of which by Lemma~\ref{lem:thin} there is exactly one. The only genuine data are the horizontal composition, which is $\ast$, and the identity $1$-cell, which is $1$.
\end{proof}

This is the precise sense in which coherence is free: the pentagon and triangle, in general substantive constraints, are automatic in a thin hom-category, and Mac Lane's conclusion holds for the trivial reason.

\begin{proposition}[Defects are visible at the $1$-cell level]\label{prop:defvis}
For all $a,b,c\in M$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $(\cell{a}\circ\cell{b})\circ\cell{c}=\cell{a}\circ(\cell{b}\circ\cell{c})$ iff $(a\ast b)\ast c=a\ast(b\ast c)$;
\item $\id_\star\circ\cell{a}=\cell{a}$ iff $1\ast a=a$;
\item $\cell{a}\circ\id_\star=\cell{a}$ iff $a\ast 1=a$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The naming map $a\mapsto\cell{a}$ is a bijection onto the set of $1$-cells and transports $\ast$ to horizontal composition, so equality of $1$-cells is equality in $M$.
\end{proof}

Thus the associator at $(a,b,c)$ is an endomorphism $2$-cell exactly at the associative triples and a $2$-cell between distinct $1$-cells exactly at the defective ones --- the germ of Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge}.

\section{What is preserved and what is forgotten}\label{sec:forget}

\begin{theorem}[Strictness criterion]\label{thm:strict}
$\Bic(M)$ is strict if and only if $M$ is a monoid, i.e.\ $(a\ast b)\ast c=a\ast(b\ast c)$ for all $a,b,c$ and $1\ast a=a=a\ast 1$ for all $a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Strictness asserts in particular the equalities of $1$-cells of Proposition~\ref{prop:defvis}, which transport to the monoid identities. ($\Leftarrow$) Given the monoid identities, those equalities of $1$-cells hold, and the identification of the associator and unitors with identity $2$-cells is an equality of parallel $2$-cells, hence automatic by Lemma~\ref{lem:thin}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $M$ has any associativity or unit defect then $\Bic(M)$ is not strict; weakness is not an artefact of the encoding. If $M$ is a monoid, $\Bic(M)$ is the classical one-object $2$-category of $M$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Every ordered pair of $1$-cells is an adjoint equivalence]\label{thm:equiv}
For any $a,b\in M$, with no algebraic relation between them, the data $f=\cell{a}$, $g=\cell{b}$ with the unique $2$-cells $\eta:\id_\star\Rightarrow g\circ f$ and $\varepsilon:f\circ g\Rightarrow\id_\star$ form an adjoint equivalence. In particular every $1$-cell is an equivalence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Unit and counit exist and are invertible by Lemma~\ref{lem:thin}, and the triangle identities are equalities of parallel $2$-cells, hence automatic.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict invertibility remembers the algebra]\label{thm:strictinv}
For $a\in M$ there is a $1$-cell $g$ with $\cell{a}\circ g=\id_\star$ and $g\circ\cell{a}=\id_\star$ \emph{as equalities of $1$-cells} if and only if there is $b\in M$ with $a\ast b=1=b\ast a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $g=\cell{b}$; the two equalities of $1$-cells are exactly $a\ast b=1$ and $b\ast a=1$, by injectivity of the naming map.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:equiv} and~\ref{thm:strictinv} are the two sides of the slogan: up to $2$-cells the bicategory knows nothing, on the nose it is the multiplication table verbatim.

\begin{theorem}[Pseudofunctoriality of arbitrary maps]\label{thm:pseudo}
Let $M,N$ be pointed magmas and $f:M\to N$ any function, not assumed to preserve $\ast$ or $1$. Then
\[
F_f:\Bic(M)\to\Bic(N),\qquad \star\mapsto\star,\quad \cell{a}\mapsto\cell{f(a)},
\]
with the unique comparison $2$-cells, is a pseudofunctor; moreover $F_{\mathrm{id}}=\mathrm{id}$ and $F_{g\circ f}=F_g\circ F_f$ on $1$-cells.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The comparison $2$-cells exist and are invertible by codiscreteness, and every compatibility axiom is an equation of parallel $2$-cells, hence automatic.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Homomorphisms are the strictly multiplicative maps]\label{thm:hom}
For $f:M\to N$ one has $F_f(\cell{a}\circ\cell{b})=F_f(\cell{a})\circ F_f(\cell{b})$ for all $a,b$, as an equality of $1$-cells, iff $f(a\ast b)=f(a)\ast f(b)$ for all $a,b$.
\end{theorem}

So the construction is defined on pointed sets and all maps, and the classical notion of homomorphism is recovered exactly as the condition for strict compatibility with horizontal composition.

\medskip

Let $\mathbf 1$ be the one-point pointed magma, $T:\Bic(M)\to\Bic(\mathbf 1)$ induced by the unique map, and $U:\Bic(\mathbf 1)\to\Bic(M)$ induced by the map sending the point to $1\in M$.

\begin{theorem}[Collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
For every $1$-cell $f$ of $\Bic(M)$ there is an invertible $2$-cell $U(T(f))\cong f$; hence at the level of $2$-isomorphism classes nothing about $M$ survives. However $U(T(\cell{a}))=\cell{a}$ as $1$-cells if and only if $a=1$. More generally, for arbitrary $g:M\to N$ and $h:N\to M$, $F_h(F_g(\cell{a}))\cong\cell{a}$ always, with equality iff $h(g(a))=a$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The associativity defect}\label{sec:defect}

Let $M$ be a finite magma with $|M|=n$.

\begin{definition}
$\DefSet(M)=\{(a,b,c)\in M^3:(a\ast b)\ast c\neq a\ast(b\ast c)\}$, $D(M)=|\DefSet(M)|$, and $A(M)=n^3-D(M)$ is the number of associative triples. The \emph{associativity density} is $d(M)=D(M)/n^3$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$D(M)=0$ iff $M$ is a semigroup, and $A(M)+D(M)=n^3$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicativity]\label{thm:mult}
For finite magmas $M,N$ with the componentwise product on $M\times N$,
\[
A(M\times N)=A(M)\,A(N),\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad 1-d(M\times N)=(1-d(M))(1-d(N)).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The bijection $((a,a'),(b,b'),(c,c'))\leftrightarrow((a,b,c),(a',b',c'))$ identifies triples in $M\times N$ with pairs of triples, and, the operation being componentwise, a triple is associative iff both components are.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Invariance]\label{thm:inv}
If $M\cong N$ as magmas then $D(M)=D(N)$; and $D(M^{\mathrm{op}})=D(M)$, where $a\ast^{\mathrm{op}}b=b\ast a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An isomorphism induces a bijection of triples preserving both bracketings. For the opposite magma, $(a\ast^{\mathrm{op}}b)\ast^{\mathrm{op}}c=c\ast(b\ast a)$ and $a\ast^{\mathrm{op}}(b\ast^{\mathrm{op}}c)=(c\ast b)\ast a$, so the reversal $(a,b,c)\mapsto(c,b,a)$ carries $\DefSet(M^{\mathrm{op}})$ bijectively onto $\DefSet(M)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Palindromes]\label{lem:pal}
If $\ast$ is commutative then $(a\ast b)\ast a=a\ast(b\ast a)$ for all $a,b$: no palindromic triple is defective.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Parity]\label{thm:parity}
The associativity defect of a finite commutative magma is even; in particular $D(M)\neq 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By commutativity the reversal $\tau(a,b,c)=(c,b,a)$ preserves defectiveness, and it is an involution whose fixed points are the palindromic triples, which by Lemma~\ref{lem:pal} are never defective. So $\tau$ acts freely on the defect set, which therefore splits into $2$-element orbits.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Defect triples avoid the unit]\label{lem:avoid}
If $1\ast a=a=a\ast 1$ for all $a$, no defect triple contains $1$ in any coordinate.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Unital bound]\label{thm:unitalbound}
A unital magma with $n$ elements satisfies $D(M)\le(n-1)^3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[Shift magma]
For a set $S$ and a self-map $\sigma:S\to S$, the \emph{shift magma} $\Sh(\sigma)$ has underlying set $S\sqcup\{1\}$, with $1$ a two-sided unit and $a\ast b=\sigma(b)$ for $a,b\in S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Maximal defect]\label{thm:shift}
If $\sigma$ has no fixed point then every triple of non-units of $\Sh(\sigma)$ is defective, so $D(\Sh(\sigma))=|S|^3=(n-1)^3$ with $n=|S|+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $a,b,c\in S$ one has $(a\ast b)\ast c=\sigma(b)\ast c=\sigma(c)$ while $a\ast(b\ast c)=a\ast\sigma(c)=\sigma(\sigma(c))$; these agree iff $\sigma$ fixes $\sigma(c)$, which is excluded. Lemma~\ref{lem:avoid} shows no other triple is defective.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every $n\ge 3$ there is a unital magma of order $n$ with $D=(n-1)^3$: take the cyclic shift $i\mapsto i+1\bmod (n-1)$, which is fixed-point-free for $n-1\ge 2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:nonpal}
For $|S|=m$, the number of triples $(a,b,c)\in S^3$ with $a\ne c$ is $m^3-m^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Commutative unital bound]\label{thm:commbound}
A commutative unital magma with $n$ elements satisfies $D(M)\le(n-1)^3-(n-1)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:avoid} the defect set lies in $S^3$ with $S=M\setminus\{1\}$, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:pal} it avoids the palindromic triples; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:nonpal}.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Negation magma]
For an abelian group $(G,+)$, the \emph{negation magma} $\Neg(G)$ has underlying set $G\sqcup\{1\}$, with $1$ a two-sided unit and $a\ast b=-(a+b)$ for $a,b\in G$. It is commutative and unital.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness in the commutative case]\label{thm:neg}
If $G$ is finite with no $2$-torsion, a triple of non-units of $\Neg(G)$ is defective iff $a\ne c$, whence $D(\Neg(G))=|G|^3-|G|^2=(n-1)^3-(n-1)^2$ for $n=|G|+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(a\ast b)\ast c=-(-(a+b)+c)=a+b-c$ and $a\ast(b\ast c)=-(a-(b+c))=-a+b+c$; these agree iff $a+a=c+c$, iff $a=c$. Count with Lemma~\ref{lem:nonpal}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every odd $m\ge3$, $G=\mathbb{Z}/m$ has no $2$-torsion, giving a commutative unital magma of order $n=m+1$ with $D=(n-1)^3-(n-1)^2$. Consistently with Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}, $m^3-m^2=m^2(m-1)$ is even.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Free unitalisation is defect-neutral]\label{thm:adjoin}
Let $\alpha^1=\alpha\sqcup\{1\}$ with $1$ a declared two-sided unit and the old product retained. Then the defect triples of $\alpha^1$ are exactly the images of those of $\alpha$, so $D(\alpha^1)=D(\alpha)$. Consequently every defect value realised by a magma of order $m$ is realised by a unital magma of order $m+1$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The bridge}\label{sec:bridge}

\begin{theorem}[Defect counts non-identity associators]\label{thm:bridge}
For a finite pointed magma $M$,
\[
\#\{(a,b,c)\in M^3:(\cell{a}\circ\cell{b})\circ\cell{c}\neq\cell{a}\circ(\cell{b}\circ\cell{c})\}=D(M).
\]
That is, the associator of $\Bic(M)$ connects distinct $1$-cells at exactly $D(M)$ triples.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Strictness $\Leftrightarrow$ zero defect]
If $M$ is finite and unital, $\Bic(M)$ is a $2$-category iff $D(M)=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Maximal weakness with perfect coherence]
For every $n\ge3$ there is a unital magma $M$ of order $n$ --- e.g.\ $\Sh(\sigma)$ for fixed-point-free $\sigma$ --- such that $\Bic(M)$ is perfectly coherent (all $2$-cells invertible, all parallel $2$-cells equal, pentagon and triangle satisfied) while every one of the $(n-1)^3$ non-unit associator instances is a genuinely non-identity $2$-cell.
\end{corollary}

Coherence and weakness are thus independent axes: a bicategory can be maximally weak and perfectly coherent at once.

\section{Exhaustive enumeration at small orders}\label{sec:enum}

Unital tables on $\{1,s_1,\dots,s_{n-1}\}$ with $1$ a declared two-sided unit are parameterised by the $(n-1)^2$ free entries, giving $n^{(n-1)^2}$ labelled tables; enumeration is feasible for $n\le4$.

\paragraph{Order 3.} Among the $81$ tables the defect distribution is
\[
D=0:11,\quad 2:14,\quad 3:8,\quad 4:18,\quad 5:12,\quad 6:12,\quad 7:4,\quad 8:2 .
\]
The maximum $8=(3-1)^3$ is attained by exactly $2$ tables --- the shift magma of the unique fixed-point-free involution of a $2$-set and its mirror. No table has $D=1$.

\paragraph{Order 4.} Among the $262\,144$ tables, exactly $156$ are associative, exactly $84$ attain $D=27$, and exactly $84$ have $D=1$; none of the latter is commutative, in agreement with Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}. Of the $84$ maximisers, only $8$ are shift magmas $a\ast b=\sigma(b)$, and $16$ counting also the mirror family $a\ast b=\sigma(a)$; up to the $3!=6$ relabellings of the non-units they fall into $18$ classes.

\paragraph{A refuted conjecture.} The order-$3$ data suggest that a unital magma can never have exactly one defect triple. This is false: the $84$ order-$4$ examples with $D=1$ refute it. The correct general statement is the parity theorem, which forbids $D=1$ only under commutativity.

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{Defect computation.} Given an $n\times n$ table $T$, compute $D=\#\{(a,b,c):T[T[a][b]][c]\neq T[a][T[b][c]]\}$ by direct triple iteration: $\Theta(n^3)$ time, $O(1)$ extra space. The same pass yields the defect set, hence the list of non-identity associator instances.

\paragraph{Exhaustive extremal search.} Enumerate the $n^{(n-1)^2}$ unital tables and tabulate defects: $\Theta(n^{(n-1)^2}\cdot n^3)$ time, feasible through $n=4$. Symmetry reduction by the $(n-1)!$ relabellings of the non-units, and an early abort once the running defect exceeds a target, extend the range.

\paragraph{Extremal constructors.} The shift magma of a fixed-point-free $\sigma$ and the negation magma of $\mathbb{Z}/m$ ($m$ odd) both build an $n\times n$ table in $\Theta(n^2)$ time, realising $D=(n-1)^3$ and $D=(n-1)^3-(n-1)^2$ respectively.

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:disc}

\paragraph{Coherence as a cheap resource.} The hardest part of exhibiting a bicategorical structure is usually the verification of coherence. The codiscrete construction shows that if one makes the hom-categories maximally uninformative, coherence costs nothing at all, whatever the algebra. It is the extreme point of a trade-off: discrete hom-categories force the strict axioms, codiscrete hom-categories force nothing, and everything interesting lies strictly between.

\paragraph{A functor of pointed sets.} Theorem~\ref{thm:pseudo} says $M\mapsto\Bic(M)$ is defined on pointed sets and all maps; Theorem~\ref{thm:hom} identifies homomorphisms as exactly the maps whose induced pseudofunctor is strictly multiplicative. Homomorphy is thus a strictness condition on an always-available pseudofunctor.

\paragraph{Measuring non-associativity.} Multiplicativity (Theorem~\ref{thm:mult}) makes the associativity density behave like a probability. Invariance (Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}) makes $D$ an invariant of the table up to relabelling and mirror symmetry, and the parity theorem shows that symmetry constrains not merely the size but the arithmetic of the failure set.

\paragraph{Limits.} Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} is a genuine caveat: nothing about $M$ survives passage to $2$-isomorphism classes. Applications must therefore work at the $1$-cell layer, where Theorems~\ref{thm:strictinv},~\ref{thm:hom} and~\ref{thm:collapse} show the algebra is fully retained.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{conjecture}[Classification of maximal-defect unital magmas]
Only $8$ of the $84$ order-$4$ maximisers come from the shift construction ($16$ counting mirrors), so the extremal family is strictly larger than the one proved sharp here. A unital magma of order $n\ge3$ should satisfy $D=(n-1)^3$ if and only if the restricted product $S\times S\to M$ (with $S$ the non-units) obeys a ``no returning to a fixed point'' condition, of which $a\ast b=\varphi(b)$ and $a\ast b=\varphi(a)$ with $\varphi$ fixed-point-free and avoiding the unit are the principal cases; the number of such tables should be $2(n-1)^{n-1}-c_n$ for an explicitly computable correction $c_n$, matching the counts $2$ at $n=3$ and $84$ at $n=4$.
\end{conjecture}

Further open problems: (i) even-order sharpness of the commutative bound, where the negation magma fails because $2$ is not invertible; (ii) interpolation between discrete and codiscrete hom-structures, asking for which ``defect graphs'' coherence remains automatic; (iii) determination of the full defect spectrum of unital magmas of order $n$ (order $3$ realises every value in $[0,8]$ except $1$; order $4$ realises $1$); (iv) higher defects, measuring the failure of the five bracketings of four elements and relating it to $D$.

\section*{Summary of main results}

\begin{itemize}
\item Every pointed magma, with no axioms, yields a one-object bicategory with codiscrete hom-category; all coherence conditions hold automatically.
\item Every $2$-cell is invertible and any two parallel $2$-cells are equal.
\item The bicategory is strict exactly when the magma is a monoid.
\item Any ordered pair of $1$-cells forms an adjoint equivalence; a $1$-cell is strictly invertible iff the element has a two-sided inverse.
\item Any set map induces a pseudofunctor, functorially; strict multiplicativity holds exactly for homomorphisms.
\item The construction retains nothing up to $2$-isomorphism, but everything on the nose.
\item $D(M)$ equals the number of non-identity associator instances; for unital $M$, strictness $\Leftrightarrow$ $D(M)=0$.
\item $A(M\times N)=A(M)A(N)$; $D$ is invariant under isomorphism, reversal and free unitalisation; $D$ is even for finite commutative magmas.
\item Sharp bounds: $D\le(n-1)^3$ for unital magmas, attained by shift magmas; $D\le(n-1)^3-(n-1)^2$ for commutative unital magmas, attained by negation magmas of $2$-torsion-free abelian groups.
\end{itemize}

